import Mathlib

/-!
This file proves that the subgroup generated by a set `S` is equal to the subgroup
generated by the union of `S` and its set of inverses `S⁻¹`. This is a foundational
result in group theory, establishing that for generation, we can either start with `S`
and allow inverses in the closure process, or start with a "symmetric" set `S ∪ S⁻¹`
and only take finite products.

The main theorem is `set_of_words_generates_group`, which is proven by assembling
three sub-lemmas. The equality `Subgroup.closure S = Subgroup.closure (S ∪ S⁻¹)`
formalizes the idea that the subgroup generated by `S` (which includes products of
elements and their inverses) is the same as the set of "words" formed from `S ∪ S⁻¹`
(which only requires products, as inverses are already in the generating set).
-/

